+# Decision: Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard keeps one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS and the server assigns each committed edit an event revision; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. How committed changes travel from the server to connected browsers — five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service — shapes three design nodes at once: what the change store must provide, what delivery operates, and how browsers recover after disconnection. All three routes can meet the 15-second freshness goal, so the choice turns on the trade between update latency, recurring cost, provider coupling, and the operational work the project takes on. Up to 800 simultaneous staff browsers and opening-time bursts of 20 edits per second bound the delivery load.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Weighted criteria:**
+
+- **Update latency (major).** How quickly a committed edit appears in connected browsers, including behavior during opening-time bursts. The 15-second goal is a floor, not the target; subsecond updates are useful.
+- **Recurring operating cost (major).** Ongoing fees or infrastructure bills attributable to the delivery route.
+- **In-house operational burden (major).** Connection and session work the project must run itself: capacity, reconnect handling, failure recovery. (From the tenet *Self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted*: neither self-hosting nor managed services is excluded on principle, so this is weighed, not assumed.)
+- **Provider coupling (moderate).** How much of the delivery path's behavior, credentials, and outage surface belongs to an outside vendor.
+- **Change store complexity (moderate).** What the delivery route obligates the store to provide — cursor queries, replayable revisions with reliable publication, or publication reconciliation.
+
+*Latent criteria considered and dropped:* implementation time (all three routes are well-trodden; none dominates) and browser compatibility (all three work in the staff browsers targeted).
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+**Description.** Browsers run a polling loop, fetching changes since their last-seen cursor every five seconds over ordinary request/response HTTPS. The server keeps no connection state; each poll is an independent query, so any request that fails is simply answered by the next poll. The store needs cursor-ordered change queries to serve "everything since revision N."
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Update latency: **partial** — seconds of lag by construction, and every browser rereads on every interval; meets the 15-second goal but not subsecond.
+- Recurring operating cost: **strong** — no fees beyond the HTTPS server already required.
+- In-house operational burden: **strong** — stateless operation, no connection capacity to manage, no reconnect protocol; a dropped browser just polls again.
+- Provider coupling: **strong** — no provider beyond the project's own HTTPS hosting.
+- Change store complexity: **partial** — cursor queries are straightforward but serve repeated reads from hundreds of browsers on every interval.
+
+**Summary.** The simplest, most forgiving route, paying in lag and repeated store reads.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+**Description.** The server pushes each committed revision to every connected browser over WebSocket connections the project operates. Browsers subscribe per event; on disconnect they reconnect and catch up by replaying revisions since the last one they received, falling back to a snapshot when the gap is too old. The store keeps revision history replayable and provides a reliable publication path (an ordered outbox) so a revision committed while a socket is briefly unavailable is not lost to it.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Update latency: **strong** — push delivery gives subsecond updates, including through bursts.
+- Recurring operating cost: **strong** — no delivery vendor; the socket server runs on the project's own infrastructure.
+- In-house operational burden: **weak** — the project owns connection capacity for hundreds of simultaneous sockets, session lifecycle, and reconnect/catch-up correctness.
+- Provider coupling: **strong** — no provider; the delivery path is entirely the project's.
+- Change store complexity: **weak** — replayable revisions plus a reliable publication path is the most demanding store contract of the three.
+
+**Summary.** Fast, vendor-free updates in exchange for owning connection and reconnect operations.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+**Description.** A managed realtime vendor (push service) carries committed revisions to browsers. The server publishes each revision to the provider; browsers subscribe through the provider's client SDK and its subscription model handles the connection. The store reconciles its publication with the provider (did every committed revision actually get published?) and the project manages provider credentials.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Update latency: **strong** — push delivery, with the provider's connection infrastructure engineered for exactly this.
+- Recurring operating cost: **weak** — recurring fees scaling with connections and messages.
+- In-house operational burden: **strong** — connection capacity and reconnect mechanics are the provider's product; the project mostly integrates.
+- Provider coupling: **weak** — delivery behavior, credentials, and the vendor's outages sit on the board's critical path.
+- Change store complexity: **partial** — publication reconciliation and credential management, against a store contract the provider partially defines.
+
+**Summary.** The fastest route to low-latency delivery, paying in recurring fees and provider dependence.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets.** It is strong on the two major criteria the choice was felt on — recurring operating cost and update latency — and strong on provider coupling, and the decision turns on the tenet *Self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted. Event updates contain stall IDs and operational statuses, not customer records*: the payload is operationally safe to carry on the project's own connections, so nothing forces a managed provider, while the tenet also made self-hosting a weighed choice rather than an assumption. The accepted cost is the criterion where it is weakest: the project takes on in-house connection capacity, session management, and reconnect/catch-up operations that polling would not need and a hosted service would absorb. That trade is taken deliberately — the board gains low-latency updates without tying its delivery path to a realtime vendor's fees or outage surface — and the visible-failure tenet (*connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board*) is reinforced by the reconnect model rather than left to a polling loop's silence.
+
+Forecloses: a stateless delivery path, and the option of simply not operating connections. Obligates: the change store must provide replayable revisions and a reliable ordered publication path; delivery must own WebSocket session and capacity management for up to 800 simultaneous browsers; browser sync must implement reconnect and catch-up from the last received revision.
